# Episode 04 – Smart Meeting Routing with IF Logic

At this stage, the workflow was already capable of collecting meeting requests, sending confirmation emails, and creating calendar events. However, every request was still being treated the same way.

In real business environments, not every meeting request requires the same action. A strategy session, for example, may need to be scheduled immediately, while a discovery call might only require an acknowledgement email before further review.

To make the workflow more intelligent, I introduced conditional logic using the IF node.

The purpose of this step was to allow the workflow to make decisions automatically based on the meeting type selected by the client.

When a client selects a Strategy Session, the workflow automatically creates a calendar event and sends a confirmation email. This ensures that high-priority consultations are scheduled immediately without requiring manual intervention.

For other meeting types such as Discovery Calls, Project Discussions, or Support Calls, the workflow follows a different path and sends a confirmation email without creating a calendar event.

This approach reflects how real businesses often operate. Different services require different processes, and automation should adapt accordingly.

One of the most valuable lessons from this episode was learning how to build decision-making logic inside n8n. Instead of creating a single linear workflow, I learned how to create multiple paths that respond differently based on client input.

From a business perspective, this makes the system more flexible, scalable, and easier to maintain as additional meeting types or scheduling rules are introduced in the future.

By the end of this episode, the workflow had evolved from a simple booking system into a smarter appointment management process capable of handling different client scenarios automatically.
